\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {CTA\xspace  on a real PushT decision.} \emph  {Top:} the context $C$ holds frozen DINOv2 features of the last two observations. The frozen diffusion policy proposes $K{=}8$ action chunks (orange: the agent's target positions over 8 steps, shown in the dashed zoomed region). In one batched pass, the world model predicts a distribution over the 16-token code of each chunk's future segment; each row shows the expected code $\hat  S^{(k)}$, one cell per token with the token's three FSQ coordinates as RGB. The reader scores each code against the goal images without seeing the action, and the highest-scoring chunk (green) is executed; a new goal requires only new reader evaluations. \emph  {Bottom:} during training, every proposal is executed from a copy of the simulator state. The actual futures of proposals 7{} and 4{} leave the block's vertices 15{} and 28{} pixels from the target pose on average. The target encoder compresses each future into a code $S$ given $C$; the reader ranks sibling codes by progress, and the decoder reconstructs the future's frozen features from $(C,S)$. The world model is trained to predict the stop-gradient code, and through the frozen reader. Frames are native $512{\times }512$ simulator renders, from which the $96{\times }96$ observations are downsampled, cropped for visibility; codes and scores are our model's outputs for this decision.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{3}{\textbf {\method on a real PushT decision.} \emph {Top:} the context $C$ holds frozen DINOv2 features of the last two observations. The frozen diffusion policy proposes $K{=}8$ action chunks (orange: the agent's target positions over 8 steps, shown in the dashed zoomed region). In one batched pass, the world model predicts a distribution over the 16-token code of each chunk's future segment; each row shows the expected code $\hat S^{(k)}$, one cell per token with the token's three FSQ coordinates as RGB. The reader scores each code against the goal images without seeing the action, and the highest-scoring chunk (green) is executed; a new goal requires only new reader evaluations. \emph {Bottom:} during training, every proposal is executed from a copy of the simulator state. The actual futures of proposals \FigBest {} and \FigWorst {} leave the block's vertices \FigErrBest {} and \FigErrWorst {} pixels from the target pose on average. The target encoder compresses each future into a code $S$ given $C$; the reader ranks sibling codes by progress, and the decoder reconstructs the future's frozen features from $(C,S)$. The world model is trained to predict the stop-gradient code, and through the frozen reader. Frames are native $512{\times }512$ simulator renders, from which the $96{\times }96$ observations are downsampled, cropped for visibility; codes and scores are our model's outputs for this decision.\relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:overview@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][2][]3}}
